_Originally posted by @JacobCoffee in https://github.com/litestar-org/litestar/discussions/4252#discussioncomment-14575383_

### Summary

In my code, I have this pattern:

```
class MyController(Controller):
    @get("/")
    def base_route(self) -> Response[bytes]:
        ...  # actual logic

    @get("/notifications")
    def notifications_route(self, request: HTMXRequest) -> Response[Any]:
        if not request.htmx:
            return Redirect(request.app.route_reverse(str(self.base_route)))`

        ...  # actual logic
```

The user visits the root route, which returns a rendered template that, among other things, lazy-loads a few sub-templates that are served through routes like `notifications_route`. These routes are meant to be accessed only through HTMX, not directly by the user. In the event that that happens, the user should be redirected back to the root route.

I want to do this in a way that is robust to changes in both the (method) name and path of the base route. I am also not really a fan of explicitly specifying a handler name, since that doesn't always play well with code analysis and automated refactoring. However, I understand (purely as an implementation detail, I believe) that `HTTPRouteHandler.__str__` generates a unique name, which leads to what I have done above. It'd be nice if I could just write `Redirect(request.app.route_reverse(self.base_route))`, which feels more intuitive to me.

Alternatively, might it make sense to set `name` in `HTTPRouteHandler.__init__` if not provided by the user...? That would be simpler, but also backwards incompatible.

### Basic Example

```
    @get("/notifications")
    def notifications_route(self, request: HTMXRequest) -> Response[Any]:
        if not request.htmx:
            return Redirect(request.app.route_reverse(self.base_route))`
```

